			<div class="content" id="post-content-3880210">
									

<p>I loved how out of control this got; the writing quality was pretty superb throughout. Part of me wished it got even more ridiculous with the extended ads; part of me appreciated the restraint.</p>
<p>The idea of a future war driven by corporations leading legions of employees/customers memetically altered for brand loyalty (and even exploiting time travel to accomplish this task better) is fantastic, and similar to ideas I've wanted to explore with ad-bots (which I find inherently terrifying).</p>
<p>I think my only serious complaint is that Miguel had too many clearly identifiable &quot;Trump-isms&quot; going on; that may vary by taste, though. My instinct tells me that you don't need the reader to positively identify who Miguel is to still enjoy the article, so you can make it more of an Easter Egg &#8212; but my instincts have been wrong, before.</p>

