				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3938622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606100952" alt="The Great Hippo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3938622)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;" >The Great Hippo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1532130697 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">20 Jul 2018 23:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3880343">
									

<p>Honestly, I'm not sure, and I'm loathe to push you either way &#8212; a lot of this stuff boils down to a confusing sort of alchemy for which there are never any clear 'right' answers. All I can tell you is how I would have done this &#8212; but I'm not at all sure how I would have done it is any better than how you <em>already</em> did it, so take the following with prescription-grade salt:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I think the meandering style of the dialogue is great, in that it works to convince us that this guy is 1) An idiot, 2) Very powerful, important, and unchallenged in his time, and 3) The natural consequence of a culture where corporatism <em>is</em> our culture.</p>
<p>(Aside: The bit where the O5 starts talking about historical figures in marketing &#8212; and Miguel instantly recognizes them, because those are the people who are considered 'historical figures' in <em>his</em> world &#8212; was an especially nice touch. In some ways, it felt a little forced, because I think it had already been well-established that this person was a product of a corporate culture &#8212; but it was still such a clever way to determine/demonstrate that. Clever enough that I'm loathe to say you should touch it or cut it.)</p>
<p>If I were writing this, I would keep that style, but remove all <em>overt</em> Trumpisms. I'd keep him talking <em>like</em> Trump, but I would write it in such a way that the reader isn't going to recognize it as Trump unless the reader is trying to recognize it as Trump (IE, nothing Trump has ever actually said, and no direct &quot;Trump-isms&quot;). I think the dialogue is strong enough to remain engaging without them (though I would possibly reduce/cut some of the fat to make it quicker &#8212; but not a lot, because I feel like the fact that he just <em>keeps going</em> is definitely part of his voice. This is clearly a guy who's accustom to being <em>heard</em>, rather than stopping to <em>listen</em>). I would then keep the black box precisely as it is &#8212; to me, that would be an Easter Egg for the reader astute enough to bother and count the number of boxes to match them to Donald Trump's name.</p>
<p>My reasoning for this: I don't think you need the reader to directly connect this to Trump. This article is definitely about a form of Trumpism, but (as counter-intuitive as this might sound) I don't think you actually need to invoke Trump to get across Trumpism. Trumpism is something that probably predates Trump by quite a large margin; he's just the most blatant and obvious example of it in our time.</p>
<p>Again, let me stress &#8212; writing is basically alchemy, not science. I think this article is great as-is, and I'm not sure if the way I would have done it is actually any better.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

